{
  "title": "{ip:È} a Maria che {pp:ho telefonato}",
  "translation": "It was Maria I phoned",
  "topics": "Word order and emphasis, punctuation in complex sentences",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "New information goes last"
      },
      "Italian word order is flexible. Normally what's already known comes first and what's new comes at the end. Moving a word changes what the sentence emphasises.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Question",
            "Natural answer"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Che cosa {pp:ha fatto} Maria?",
              "Maria {pp:ha telefonato}."
            ],
            [
              "Chi {pp:ha telefonato}?",
              "{pp:Ha telefonato} Maria."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Three ways of moving the focus"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Name",
            "How it works",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Cleft sentence",
              "è + the focus + che",
              "{ip:È} a Maria che {pp:ho telefonato}."
            ],
            [
              "Moving to the front",
              "topic first, pronoun repeats it",
              "Il pane l'{pp:ho comprato} io."
            ],
            [
              "Moving to the end",
              "pronoun first, topic after it",
              "L'{pp:ho comprato} io, il pane."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "In a cleft sentence, a preposition stays with the focus: *{ip:È} di questo che {im:volevo} parlarti*. When a direct object is moved in front of the verb, a pronoun must repeat it: *La pizza la {ip:prendo} io*. Without the pronoun, the sentence sounds wrong in everyday use.",
      {
        "h": "When is a comma needed?"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Rule",
            "Right",
            "Wrong"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Never between subject and verb",
              "Mia sorella {ip:abita} a Roma.",
              "Mia sorella, {ip:abita} a Roma."
            ],
            [
              "Never between verb and object",
              "{pp:Ho comprato} il pane.",
              "{pp:Ho comprato}, il pane."
            ],
            [
              "Before ma, però, tuttavia",
              "{ip:È} tardi, ma {ip:esco}.",
              "{ip:È} tardi ma, {ip:esco}."
            ],
            [
              "After a subordinate clause at the start",
              "Se {ip:piove}, {ip:resto}.",
              "Se {ip:piove} {ip:resto},"
            ],
            [
              "Around extra information",
              "Mio padre, che {ip:è} medico, {ip:lavora}.",
              "Mio padre che {ip:è} medico, {ip:lavora}."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Relative clauses and commas"
      },
      "A comma changes the meaning. *I ragazzi, che {im:erano} stanchi, {pp:sono andati} a letto* means all the boys were tired. *I ragazzi che {im:erano} stanchi {pp:sono andati} a letto* means only some went: those who were tired.",
      {
        "h": "Colon and semicolon"
      },
      "A colon (:) introduces an explanation, a list or a quotation: *{pp:Ho capito} una cosa: non {ip:devo} arrendermi.* A semicolon (;) separates two independent but closely linked thoughts: *Lui {pp:è partito}; lei {pp:è rimasta}.*"
    ]
  },
